@page
@model PokeBinder.Pages.Admin.SetRarityModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Rarity weights";
    Layout = "_AdminLayout";
}

<div class="max-w-5xl">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-900">Rarity weights</h1>

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-neutral-600">
        A rarity is not worth the same in every set, and its name does not say what it is worth. A
        Special Illustration Rare is around 70 packs of opening in ME: Ascended Heroes and around
        20 in the 30th anniversary set -- the same rarity name, three and a half times the effort.
        These two numbers are what a binder is sorted by, and they are entered per set because that
        is the only level at which either of them is true.
    </p>

    <dl class="mt-6 space-y-3 text-sm text-neutral-600">
        <div>
            <dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-800">Pull rate</dt>
            <dd>
                Roughly how many packs of this set it takes before you are likely to have pulled
                that rarity. Bigger is harder, so bigger is rarer, and the unit is the same in
                every set -- which is what makes 540 here and 20 there comparable. Round to whole
                packs, and let two rarities share a number where that is the truth: anything you
                pull most packs is a 1.
            </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-800">Name order</dt>
            <dd>
                A ranking by what the rarity is <em>called</em>, deliberately not by how hard it is
                to pull, so that a sort can keep all the Special Illustration Rares together
                instead of scattering them by how generous each set was. Numbered the same way
                round as the pull rate: Common low, Special Illustration Rare high.
            </dd>
        </div>
    </dl>

    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-neutral-600">
        Both are hand-entered and both may be left empty, which means the rarity is unweighted.
        Two rarities in a set may share a pull rate, because two rarities really can take about
        the same number of packs -- but not a name order, which is a ranking, and a ranking that
        repeats itself does not say which of the two comes first. The card count beside each row
        is context for the guess, not a weight.
    </p>

    @*
        One form around the editor: method="post" is what makes Razor emit the antiforgery field,
        and the save button posts through htmx, which serialises this form. Without it the save
        would be a 400.

        The form is outside the swapped element on purpose -- htmx replaces the editor on every
        select, save and refusal, and the token has to survive all three.
    *@
    <form method="post" class="mt-8">
        @*
            The string attribute is written with an @ and the others are not, which is the tag
            helper rule rather than a style: a string-typed attribute is taken as the literal text
            unless it is an expression, so "Model.Saved" without one renders those nine characters
            in the banner.
        *@
        <rarity-set-editor sets="Model.Sets" selected="Model.Selected" errors="Model.Errors" saved="@Model.Saved" />
    </form>
</div>
